\begin_layout Section
Summary of literature review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Inequality - is relevant to education - we only seem to argue that increasing education access would improve social conditions - but given that a lot of education is signaling,
 incrasing signaling itself may not increase welfare as much.
 the presented attempts to link the effects of inequality and effort to understand the policy implications for education.
 That results demonstrate how equality of opportunity may come in conflict with equality of outcome is (see Heckman,
 Mosso - - THE ECONOMICS OF HUMAN DEVELOPMENT AND SOCIAL MOBILITY - https://www.nber.org/system/files/working_papers/w19925/w19925.pdf ).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An early remark of the role of signaling in education is from Spence.
 More recent literature e.g.
 from Alessa K.
 Durst,
 Jaege (Sheepskin) also highlights the signaling role in educaiton.
 Heisig notes that the relationship between formal qualifications and labor market outcomes is stronger in some countries than in others and that the strength of the association is related to secondary education system.
 Here,
 the signaling theory sits in contrast with human capital theory of education.
 While Spence emphasises the the role of relative positio,
 he does not rely on the information asymmetry inherent in institutions such as education,
 where inherent talents are only revealed through competitions.
 In what Jenck details,
 as well as in a Weberian framework,
 these competitions are an interplay of both social participation and innate characteristics.
 Thurow adresses the problem of inequality relates disparities in social capital and emphasises how a view of relation positioning of labour participants (labour queue) could be more relevant to social positions than a market of skills (more recent literature instrument view also reiterates this view).
 As Thurow also remarks,
 the maximisation of risk-neutral values seems to be the accepted goal from Rawls.
 The key insight offered from Thurow is how workers compete not based on willingness to accept low wages but for position based on background characteristics.
 This is expanded upon by the presented model by allowing both uncertainty and combination of raw talent and social capital to have a role in economic success.
 The combination accounts for how tracked systems deprive low-achieving students of stimulating interactions with higher-achieving peers and thereby reinforce preexisting inequalities (Gamoran 2000).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The presented model deals with how uncertainty and perception of risk also are related with social capital investments.
 How wealth and status positions are maintained is neither purely hereditary nor market-driven.
 Thurow addresses this point and highlights,
 like Weber,
 how social capital investments affect the queue of relative positions - entailing social processes are notdetached from economic processes.
 That consumer optimise value at risk allows us to link social and economic goals.
 The uncertainty is subject to long-term constraints and whether such competitions are feasilble or not have important constraints for participation costs and inequality.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Social capital itself has had a long and rich literature.
 recent empirical studies have also highlighted that education is positional (Jaege,
 durst,
 Hungerford-solon).
 But issues exist in how empirical studies study the effect of social capital.
 It is common for the literature to understand social capital as network effects - e.g.
 by exploring the effect of specific skills that having more money from parents can help with.
 Camboni,
 Niu,
 Pai,
 Vohra - explore the education- and detail how more informative monitoring policies appeal to high-type students seeking to differentiate themselves from low-type students through increased effort.
 A combination of both talent and social capital is thus understood to affect future success of individuals.
 That these effects are both economic and social allows us to understand how long-term inequality may interact with social capital investment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The contribution from the model presented in the paper is its relation with economics of inequality.
 Lowering of participation costs could increase competition - but first,
 competitions among the poor too are affected when social capital investments become more important.
 Second,
 higher income differences can prevail with lower participation costs.
 It may be worthwhile to consider whether lower income differencs (which have implications more wide-ranging than education) are more important to address than higher participation costs.
 Participation costs serve a different role in maintaining status comepetitions.
 The extreme situation where income differences are lower but participation costs higher could be more desirable in certain situations than were income differences are high but participation costs are lower.
 This is where the reference-dependent utility server as an important policy goal - of letting social rankings serve local goals and not allow poor and rich to share the same utility from wealth.
 The results also shows how equality of opportunity may come in conflict with equality of outcome .
 
\end_layout

